#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054514 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054716 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70b2f3c7-1600-51c6-91ad-855bcee7d18a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Point on the Unit Circle Given Its Central Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "c4d9963b-1fed-523f-89c7-961ffb12feee" :question/math-academy-id "e-3343" :question/problem "![](images/3f/3f162b6b0c51a94c3c752f8a6ee59d4232c5bbb2fe619c7c6273f37805c281b0.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. What are the coordinates of the point $P$?" :question/worked-solution "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\n\\theta=45^\\circ\n$$\n\nat the point $P$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\cos 45^{∘} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\\\\ny &= \\sin 45^{∘} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2},\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054515 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054717 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "568011de-56c0-55f4-95ec-0a426cbf6b67" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Central Angle Given the Coordinates of a Point on the Unit Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "59696ddf-7aa4-561a-ac77-2216038294b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-3346" :question/problem "![](images/56/56adc0846dcc0a0f6352eb4ce411f4fffdcd6f91e20524c83bd70af1bd4d948c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}$, what is the measure of the angle $\\theta$?" :question/worked-solution "We're given that\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\n$$\n\nat the point $P$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\n$$\n\nWe compute the angle $\\theta$ using the inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arcsin\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\\right) = 60^\\circ\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054516 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054719 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83a4297b-016b-5182-bf4f-cd2b64a23c41" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent or Cotangent of a Central Angle Given a Point on the Unit Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "ee188df6-0ef5-5819-8fd9-77d48e8a5dce" :question/math-academy-id "e-3347" :question/problem "![](images/1b/1b084e0ad229e935d70d751f0e0ddaacf5832017951a7ef8e334f27834662d5c.png)\n\nThe point $P\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 7}{7},\\dfrac{\\sqrt{42}}{7}\\right)$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\tan\\theta$?" :question/worked-solution "We're given that\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 7}{7}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{42}}{7}\n$$\n\nat the point $P$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 7}{7}, \\qquad \\sin\\theta =\\dfrac{\\sqrt{42}}{7}\n$$\n\nKnowing that\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin \\theta}{\\cos\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $\\tan\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{42}}{7}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 7}{7}\\right)} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{42}}{\\sqrt{7}} = \\sqrt 6\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054517 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054718 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8487f45b-a734-5375-abc2-5a347d49587c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratio Given the Coordinates of a Point on the Unit Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "acb093c8-daa1-5eee-8678-e985fb9dfd71" :question/math-academy-id "e-3348" :question/problem "![](images/16/1635b5c2b1c515601e5339a0814132fca63164e7ab2b45c93ea5c2b3f5e0358b.png)\n\nThe point $P\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 8}{3},\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\sec\\theta$?" :question/worked-solution "We're given that\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 8}{3}\n$$\n\nat the point $P$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 8}{3}\n$$\n\nKnowing that\n\n$$\n\\sec\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $\\sec\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sec\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 8}{3}\\right)} =\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt 8} = \\dfrac{3\\sqrt 8}{8}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054717 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "568011de-56c0-55f4-95ec-0a426cbf6b67" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Central Angle Given the Coordinates of a Point on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbf3e945-915f-505c-8eba-a8b885843931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053914} {:answer/id #uuid "bf79e7fe-af5c-50a2-a68d-090ec078fb45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053915} {:answer/id #uuid "e3c498ac-930e-5a47-94ab-0d0db471847f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053916} {:answer/id #uuid "f2e00890-fb9e-5664-b8da-34bd2c52b2bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053917} {:answer/id #uuid "16e8c52d-f083-58c5-9b57-eb7f41ca4a5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053918}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ad7b8292-2cc9-59fa-8427-59bb85b1cab1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053913}] :question/id #uuid "58adfdfb-8a6a-5b73-a9ed-33d397b103fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-64339" :question/problem "![](images/19/198f3a8a98e9650cf53c81a24a4fc96e08a743bdd6c48e47eb4b59f5bc8e4f2c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$, what is the measure of the angle $θ$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to a